{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"01","chapter_title":"GitHub Pages Personal Website","title":"Website Purpose and Information Architecture","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting website purpose and information architecture to GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat100-supplement-06-01-lo-01","statement":"Write a one-sentence purpose statement ([audience] + [single job]) and use it to rank candidate pages"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-06-01-lo-02","statement":"Apply the task, reach, and empty checks to keep, promote, or cut each candidate page within the click budget"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-06-01-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-06-01-lo-04","statement":"Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it"}],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website","cat100_supplement_website_purpose_and_information_architecture","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":1,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/05/08"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_6_1_website_purpose_and_information_architecture_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_6_1_website_purpose_and_information_architecture_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_6_1_website_purpose_and_information_architecture_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_6_1_website_purpose_and_information_architecture_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"cat100-supplement-06-01-lo-01":["cat100_supplement_6_1_website_purpose_and_information_architecture_artifact_signal"],"cat100-supplement-06-01-lo-02":["cat100_supplement_6_1_website_purpose_and_information_architecture_calibration"],"cat100-supplement-06-01-lo-03":["cat100_supplement_6_1_website_purpose_and_information_architecture_ai_boundary"],"cat100-supplement-06-01-lo-04":["cat100_supplement_6_1_website_purpose_and_information_architecture_annotation_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Website Purpose and Information Architecture\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Website Purpose And Information Architecture, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/06-01-website-purpose-and-information-architectu.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Website Purpose and Information Architecture: Website Purpose And Information Architecture, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nEvery page you will publish in this module begins with two questions that most beginners skip: who is going to look at this, and what do I want them to do next? Information architecture (often shortened to IA) is the answer to the second question made visible as structure. It is the decision about which pages exist, what each page is named, and how a visitor moves between them. A portfolio with five well-chosen pages and clear navigation does its job; a portfolio with eleven pages, three of them empty, sends the visitor into a maze and quietly undermines the work it was meant to showcase.\n\nThis section comes first because the file tree you build in 06.03 and the HTML you write in 06.02 are downstream of the IA decision. If you write markup before you decide what the site is for, you end up styling pages no one needs. Get the architecture right and the rest of the module is mostly execution.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Write a one-sentence purpose statement ([audience] + [single job]) and use it to rank candidate pages.\n- Apply the task, reach, and empty checks to keep, promote, or cut each candidate page within the click budget.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA first-year student opens her portfolio repo and starts adding pages the way she would add tabs to a notebook: Home, About, Projects, Resume, Contact, Blog, Hobbies, Photos, Favorite Links, Guestbook, Now Playing. Eleven files. When a peer reviewer visits, he clicks Blog (empty), then Photos (one selfie), then gives up before finding the one dashboard project that actually demonstrates her skill. The site is not broken; it is undifferentiated. Nothing tells the visitor where the evidence is.\n\nThe problem is not effort, it is the absence of a purpose statement that could have ranked the pages. This section gives you the tool to make that ranking before you write a single line of HTML, so that every page you keep earns its place against a stated goal.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nInformation architecture rests on one principle: structure should follow the visitor's task, not the author's inventory of things they could mention. Three ideas make that principle usable.\n\n**Purpose statement.** Write one sentence in the form \"This site helps [specific audience] [do one thing].\" For a job-seeking portfolio: \"This site helps a hiring manager confirm in under a minute that I can build working data tools.\" That sentence is a filter. Any page that does not serve it is a candidate for cutting.\n\n**Navigation depth and the click budget.** Visitors abandon sites whose navigation gives weak cues about where the content lives. The practical default for a small portfolio is that any important page is reachable in one click from Home (depth 1), not because clicks are costly in themselves, but because every extra hop is another chance for a vague label to lose the visitor. If your most impressive project takes three clicks to reach (Home, then Projects, then a category, then the project), you have spent your visitor's patience on hierarchy they did not ask for. Flat beats deep for a five-to-seven-page site because each link can carry a strong, specific label.\n\n> **Recent evidence.** The popular \"three-click rule\" is a heuristic, not a hard limit. Nielsen Norman Group's review of the evidence (Laubheimer, 2019) found that user drop-off and satisfaction do not hinge on a raw click count. What governs abandonment is information scent: the cues in link labels and surrounding context that tell a visitor a path leads to their goal (Budiu, 2020; Pirolli, 2005). Users tolerate more clicks when each click has strong scent and leave when scent is weak, regardless of depth, which is why the Labeling principle below does as much work as the depth target.\n\n**Labeling.** Page names are an interface. \"Projects\" tells a visitor what they will find; \"Stuff\" or \"My Work Maybe\" does not. Use the words your audience already uses. A hiring manager searches for \"Resume,\" not \"Curriculum Vitae of My Journey.\"\n\n<inline-check question=\"A hiring-manager portfolio buries its best dashboard project three clicks deep under Home, then Projects, then a Data category. Which IA principle does this violate?\" explanation=\"The click budget (navigation depth) target for a small portfolio is that any important page is reachable in one click from Home. Burying the best project three clicks deep spends the visitor's patience on hierarchy. The fix is to promote that project toward Home, not to add labels or pages.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The click budget: the most important page should be reachable in about one click from Home\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The purpose statement: the site needs more pages to look complete\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Labeling: the category just needs a clearer name\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nTwo course anchors do real work here. [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined) frames the audience as a professional evaluator with limited time, which is why the click budget matters. [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students) reminds you that an empty or abandoned page is itself a signal: it tells visitors the author does not maintain what they publish.\n\nThe table below shows the same content organized two ways. The right column is not prettier; it is structured around the purpose statement.\n\n| Author-centered (inventory) | Visitor-centered (task) |\n| --- | --- |\n| Home, About, Blog, Photos, Projects, Hobbies, Resume, Links, Contact | Home, Projects, Resume, About, Contact |\n| Best project is 3 clicks deep | Best project is 1 click from Home |\n| Two empty pages signal neglect | Every page has real content |\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA student is planning the information architecture for a portfolio aimed at one audience: hiring managers for entry-level data roles. Watch the structure move from a dumped list to a navigable map.\n\n**Step 1. Name the audience and the one job.** Audience: hiring manager scanning for evidence I can do the work. Job: reach a relevant project and my resume in under 30 seconds.\n\n**Step 2. List candidate pages, then cut to fit the job.**\n\n| Candidate page | Keep? | Reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Home | Keep | Orients and routes to the rest |\n| About | Keep | One paragraph plus photo, builds trust |\n| Projects | Keep | The evidence the audience came for |\n| Resume | Keep | A reviewer expects to download it |\n| Contact | Keep | The conversion step |\n| Blog | Cut | No posts yet; an empty page reads as neglect |\n| Guestbook | Cut | Serves no hiring-manager goal |\n\n**Step 3. Write the file tree that this map implies.**\n\n```text\nmy-portfolio/\n  index.html        (Home: one-line intro plus links to Projects, Resume)\n  about.html        (About: short bio)\n  projects/\n    index.html      (project cards, one link each)\n    dashboard.html  (one project detail page)\n  resume.html       (or links to resume.pdf)\n  assets/\n    style.css\n    resume.pdf\n```\n\n**Step 4. Justify one cut in writing.** \"I removed Blog because it would have been empty. An empty section signals an unfinished site, which works against the trust the About page is trying to build.\"\n\nNotice the shift: the architecture is now a path, not a pile. Every kept page maps to the audience's job, and the one removal is documented as a decision rather than an accident.\n\nReconstruct the order of the four IA planning moves the worked example used. Each step depends on the one before it, so getting the sequence right is the skill.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Name the one audience and the single job they came to do\",\"List every candidate page you could build\",\"Cut each page that fails the task, reach, or empty check\",\"Write the file tree the surviving pages imply\",\"Justify the hardest cut in one written sentence\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nKeep a page only if you can name the visitor task it serves and that task ranks against your purpose statement. Apply this test to every candidate page before it enters the file tree:\n\n1. **Task check:** What does a visitor come to this page to do? If you cannot name the task in one verb phrase (\"download my resume,\" \"see a working project\"), the page is decoration.\n2. **Reach check:** How many clicks from Home? If an important page is deeper than one click, ask whether the hierarchy is for the visitor or for you.\n3. **Empty check:** Does the page have real content today? An empty page is worse than a missing one because it signals abandonment.\n\nA page that fails the task check should be cut. A page that fails the reach check should be promoted toward Home. A page that fails the empty check should be removed until it has content.\n\nWalk one candidate page through the three checks and predict the verdict before you reveal it.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Apply the three checks to a candidate Blog page (portfolio for a hiring manager, no posts written yet)\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Task check first: can you name in one verb phrase what a hiring manager comes to a Blog page to do?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes: read my published technical writing\",\"No clear hiring-manager task, and there are no posts anyway\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"There is no posted writing, so no verb-phrase task survives. The page is decoration relative to the stated audience.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Empty check next: does the page have real content today?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes, it has a welcome placeholder\",\"No, it is empty\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"An empty page is worse than a missing one because it signals abandonment to a recruiter.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Verdict: cut the Blog page now. It fails both the task check and the empty check. Document the cut in one sentence so it reads as a decision, not an oversight: I removed Blog because it would have been empty, and an empty section signals neglect.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n> **Accessibility of your IA.** The labeling principle is also an accessibility requirement, which is what makes your artifact an accessibility-checked file tree rather than just a tidy one. Descriptive page names double as accessible link text: [WCAG 2.4.4 Link Purpose (In Context), Level A](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/link-purpose-in-context.html) asks that a link's purpose be clear from its text alone, which \"Projects\" satisfies and \"Stuff\" does not. A shallow, consistent nav with a skip-to-content link and proper landmark and heading structure is what lets screen-reader users jump straight to a section (WCAG 2.4.1 Bypass Blocks, Level A), and WCAG 2.4.6 Headings and Labels (Level AA) requires that those headings and labels describe their topic or purpose. Run this one IA-level check before you write any HTML in 06.02: read each page name aloud and ask whether it would still orient a visitor if it were the only thing a screen reader announced.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common mistake is treating \"more pages\" as \"more impressive.\" A visitor judges a portfolio by the strongest thing they can find quickly, not by the total count of links. Adding a thin Hobbies page does not raise the ceiling; it lowers the floor by diluting the path to your best work.\n\nA second misreading is confusing visual polish with architecture. You can apply a beautiful stylesheet to a site whose navigation still hides the resume three clicks down. Polish is the last layer; structure is the first.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the rule to a concrete case. A student is building a site to apply for a summer data internship and a campus tutoring job. He drafts the purpose statement first: \"This site helps an internship recruiter and a tutoring coordinator quickly confirm I can analyze data and explain it clearly.\" Notice the statement names two audiences, which means the architecture must serve both without forcing either to dig.\n\nHe lists candidate pages and runs the task check. Projects (serves the recruiter: shows analysis), Tutoring Experience (serves the coordinator: shows teaching), Resume (serves both), About (builds trust for both), Contact (the conversion step for both). A Blog page fails the empty check (no posts) and is cut. A Photos page fails the task check (neither audience needs it) and is cut.\n\nHe then runs the reach check. His best dashboard project was going to live under Projects, then under a \"Data\" subcategory, two clicks deep. Because the recruiter is the higher-stakes audience, he promotes that single project to a featured card directly on Home, keeping it one click away. The hierarchy now bends toward the visitor with the least patience and the most influence.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader does not just accept the example site; they can regenerate the reasoning. As you study the worked example below, keep a two-column note: in the left column write each kept or cut page, and in the right column write the exact task or failed check that justified the decision. If you can reconstruct the full IA from your notes without rereading the prose, you understand the section.\n\nA strong reader of website IA can answer four questions about any site:\n\n1. What single sentence states this site's purpose, and which audience does it name?\n2. Which page is the visitor's first goal, and how many clicks away is it?\n3. Which existing page would a skeptical reviewer call filler, and why?\n4. If the audience changed (say, from a recruiter to a graduate admissions committee), which pages would you reorder or rename?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour file tree is strong when a stranger can infer your purpose statement just by reading the page names and following the navigation. If they have to guess who the site is for, the labeling or the structure is doing too little work. Write your purpose statement at the top of your planning notes, then check that every page name and every link visibly serves it.\n\nA strong submission includes the one cut you found hardest to make and the reason you made it. For example: \"I removed the Blog page even though I liked the idea, because an empty Blog signals neglect to a recruiter, and that risk outweighed the chance I might post later.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same portfolio to a different audience: a graduate program in statistics rather than an industry internship. The purpose statement changes (\"help an admissions reviewer judge my research potential\"), and that single change cascades through the architecture. Projects stays but is reframed around method and rigor rather than business impact. A Coursework or Research page, useless to a recruiter, now earns a top-level spot. Contact becomes less central; a clear academic email is enough. The HTML and the file mechanics are identical; the architecture is different because the visitor's task is different. If you can perform that re-ranking deliberately, you understand IA as a decision rather than a template.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/06-01-website-purpose-and-information-architectu-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"06.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted. If you ask a current assistant such as ChatGPT, Gemini, or Claude to propose a sitemap, treat the proposal as a draft to judge against your purpose statement, and record in your AI-use note which tool (and version, if you know it) you used: model behavior and naming change often, so the disclosure is part of the evidence.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks for CAT 100 06.01. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_website_purpose_and_information_architecture\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links claim, evidence, tool boundary, revision, and remaining limitation. That is what separates digital fluency from simple completion.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_website_purpose_and_information_architecture\", \"cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website\", \"evidence_based_revision\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n- Laubheimer, P. (2019). The 3-click rule for navigation is false. Nielsen Norman Group. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/3-click-rule/\n- Budiu, R. (2020). Information scent: How users decide where to go next. Nielsen Norman Group. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/information-scent/\n- Pirolli, P. (2005). Rational analyses of information foraging on the web. *Cognitive Science, 29*(3). https://doi.org/10.1207/s15516709cog0000_20\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_1_website_purpose_and_information_architecture_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_website_purpose_and_information_architecture","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_website_purpose_and_information_architecture","cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website"],"stem":"You are sketching the page list for a one-person portfolio site aimed at hiring managers. Which top-level navigation best matches that single audience and purpose?","options":["Home, About, Projects, Resume, Contact","Home, Blog, Photo Gallery, Fan Mail, Guestbook, Webring","index.html only, with every section stacked and no links","A single Downloads page that links to a 40 MB ZIP of everything"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A hiring-manager audience needs to reach evidence of your work fast: a short, predictable set of pages (About, Projects, Resume, Contact) maps directly to what they came to verify.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_1_more_pages_is_better","difficulty":"easy","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-06-01-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_1_website_purpose_and_information_architecture_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_website_purpose_and_information_architecture","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_website_purpose_and_information_architecture","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Your project page currently reads: 'Stuff I made.' Which heading is the strongest information-architecture revision for a reader scanning for relevant work?","options":["Things","Selected Projects: Data Dashboards and Web Builds","Click Here","Untitled Section 2"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A descriptive heading that names the category of work lets a scanner predict the content and decide to read on. 'Stuff' and 'Things' carry no orienting information.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_1_label_clarity_optional","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-06-01-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_1_website_purpose_and_information_architecture_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat100_supplement_website_purpose_and_information_architecture"],"stem":"You ask a current AI assistant (such as ChatGPT, Gemini, or Claude) to propose a sitemap. It returns Home, About, Services, Pricing, Team, Careers. What is the right next move for your personal student portfolio?","options":["Adopt the full sitemap because the AI is more experienced at web design","Keep only the pages that fit a single-author student portfolio (drop Services, Pricing, Team, Careers) and justify the cut","Add even more pages so the site looks like a real company","Reject the whole idea of a sitemap because AI suggested it"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The AI proposal is a draft for a business, not a student. You judge it against your purpose and audience, keep the fitting pages, and document why you removed the rest.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_1_ai_sitemap_as_authority","difficulty":"hard","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-06-01-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_1_website_purpose_and_information_architecture_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_website_purpose_and_information_architecture","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_website_purpose_and_information_architecture"],"stem":"A visitor lands on your Projects page from a search result. Which structure best supports their need to reach one specific project quickly?","options":["One long page where all six projects run together with no headings or anchors","Each project as a card with a title, one-line summary, and a link to its own detail page","A page that auto-plays a slideshow of all projects with no way to pause","A page listing project titles only, with the details available by emailing you"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Cards with titles, summaries, and per-project links let a visitor scan, predict, and jump straight to the one project they want, which is the core job of information architecture.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_1_more_pages_is_better","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-06-01-lo-02"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_1_more_pages_is_better","pattern":"more_pages_equals_better_site","description":"Believing a personal site is improved by adding more pages and sections rather than by matching pages to a single audience and purpose","trigger":"learner proposes blog, gallery, guestbook, and other pages a hiring-manager audience did not need","feedback":"Start from one audience and one goal. Keep only the pages that help that reader reach the evidence they came for. Extra pages dilute the path, they do not strengthen it.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_1_label_clarity_optional","pattern":"vague_navigation_labels","description":"Using vague labels like Stuff, Things, or Click Here instead of descriptive labels that let a visitor predict the content","trigger":"learner names a section with a non-orienting word","feedback":"Rename the label so a scanning reader can predict what is behind it. A good label answers What will I find here before the click.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_1_ai_sitemap_as_authority","pattern":"ai_sitemap_as_authority","description":"Adopting an AI-generated sitemap wholesale even when it was built for a business rather than a single-author student portfolio","trigger":"learner keeps AI-suggested pages such as Pricing or Careers that do not fit the actual purpose","feedback":"Treat the AI sitemap as a proposal. Judge each page against your audience and purpose, keep what fits, and write down why you cut the rest.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"0360c0624b3643e95d512ca98097c432a61bf13cb5c1a159c8fb26875807d133","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}